To manipulate an application properties variable when using Mockito and JUnit 5 in Java Spring Boot testing, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a configuration class that uses the `@ConfigurationProperties` annotation to bind the properties to a Java object.
2. Use Mockito to mock the configuration class and set the desired property value.
3. Inject the mocked configuration class into your test case and verify that the property value was set correctly.

Here's an example:

1. Create a configuration class:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "myapp")
public class MyAppProperties {
    private String myProperty;

    // Getters and setters
    public String getMyProperty() {
        return myProperty;
    }

    public void setMyProperty(String myProperty) {
        this.myProperty = myProperty;
    }
}
```

2. Use Mockito to mock the configuration class and set the desired property value:

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.mockito.Mockito;

import static org.mockito.ArgumentMatchers.anyString;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.when;

public class MyAppPropertiesTest {

    @Test
    public void testMyProperty() {
        // Create a mock instance of MyAppProperties
        MyAppProperties mockMyAppProperties = Mockito.mock(MyAppProperties.class);

        // Set the desired property value
        when(mockMyAppProperties.getMyProperty()).thenReturn("my-value");

        // Inject the mocked configuration class into your test case
        MyAppPropertiesProperties properties = new MyAppPropertiesProperties(mockMyAppProperties);

        // Verify that the property value was set correctly
        assertEquals("my-value", properties.getMyProperty());
    }
}
```

In this example, the `MyAppPropertiesProperties` class is a wrapper class that allows you to inject the `MyAppProperties` instance into your test case. You can then use the `getMyProperty()` method to retrieve the value of the `myProperty` variable and verify that it was set correctly.

Note: You need to include the appropriate dependencies in your `pom.xml` or `build.gradle` file to use Mockito and JUnit 5.

To create a basic HTML file with a black background, a silver sidebar, and sample text that you can easily edit, you can use the following HTML and CSS code. This example includes a collapsible sidebar using a simple JavaScript function.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Sample Page with Sidebar</title>
    <style>
        body {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            background-color: black;
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            height: 100vh;
        }
        .sidebar {
            background-color: silver;
            width: 200px;
            height: 100%;
            position: fixed;
            left: 0;
            top: 0;
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            justify-content: space-between;
        }
        .content {
            margin-left: 200px;
            flex-grow: 1;
        }
        .sidebar h2 {
            color: white;
            font-size: 1.5em;
            padding: 10px;
        }
        .sidebar button {
            background-color: black;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            padding: 10px;
            cursor: pointer;
            margin-top: 10px;
        }
        .sidebar button:hover {
            background-color: gray;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="sidebar">
        <h2>Sidebar</h2>
        <button onclick="toggleSidebar()">Toggle Sidebar</button>
        <p>Sample text in the sidebar.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content">
        <h1>Content Area</h1>
        <p>This is the main content area. Feel free to edit this text.</p>
    </div>

    <script>
        function toggleSidebar() {
            var sidebar = document.querySelector('.sidebar');
            sidebar.style.width = sidebar.style.width === '200px' ? '0' : '200px';
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code creates a simple HTML page with a black background and a fixed sidebar on the left. The sidebar can be toggled to collapse and expand using a button. The content area on the right can be edited easily. Feel free to modify the text and styles as needed.

