    let mut string_vector: Vec<String> = vec!["apple".to_string(), "banana".to_string(), "cherry".to_string()];

    // You can also create an empty vector and add elements to it later
    let mut empty_vector: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();
    empty_vector.push(10);
    empty_vector.push(20);

    // Print the vectors
    println!("Integer vector: {:?}", int_vector);
    println!("String vector: {:?}", string_vector);
    println!("Empty vector after adding elements: {:?}", empty_vector);
}
```

In this example, `int_vector` is created with initial values, `string_vector` is created with initial string values, and `empty_vector` is created empty and then populated with integers. The `println!` macro is used to print the contents of the vectors.

