To ensure that the `valueString` can be successfully deserialized to an `IEnumerable<string>`, you can write a check using reflection or type checking before executing the deserialization code. Here is an example of how you can implement this check in C#:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        string valueString = "[\"value1\", \"value2\"]"; // Example input

        if (CanDeserializeToIEnumerableString(valueString))
        {
            IEnumerable<string> result = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<IEnumerable<string>>(valueString);
            // Proceed with using the result
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("The provided value string cannot be deserialized to IEnumerable<string>");
        }
    }

    private static bool CanDeserializeToIEnumerableString(string valueString)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(valueString))
        {
            return false;
        }

        try
        {
            // Try to deserialize the value string to a generic object
            dynamic dynamicValue = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject(valueString);
            // Check if the deserialized value is of IEnumerable type
            return dynamicValue is IEnumerable<string>;
        }
        catch (JsonException)
        {
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

In this code:
1. The `CanDeserializeToIEnumerableString` method checks if the `valueString` can be deserialized to an `IEnumerable<string>`.
2. It first checks if the `valueString` is null or empty.
3. It then tries to deserialize the `valueString` to a dynamic object and checks if the result is of the `IEnumerable<string>` type.
4. If deserialization fails (throws a `JsonException`), it returns false.

This way, you can ensure that the code line `JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<IEnumerable<string>>(valueString);` is only executed when the `valueString` is valid for deserialization to `IEnumerable<string>`.

